#!/usr/local/bin/dyalogscript
⍝ Shell against a real, non-JSON, line-oriented interpreter: sqlite3
⍝ (ADR D20; Tutorial step 1b; examples/sqlite-repl.apls is the same
⍝ thing without assertions).
⍝
⍝ Why this is worth a test rather than just an example: every other test
⍝ in this suite drives Shell through a JSON-RPC peer, so nothing
⍝ actually PROVED the claim ADR D1/D5 and the Tutorial both make — that
⍝ Shell is protocol-agnostic and would work the same against anything
⍝ line-oriented. This does, and pins three behaviours that a JSON-RPC
⍝ peer never exercises:
⍝   - one Send producing MANY response lines (the caller frames the
⍝     answer itself; there is no id to correlate on),
⍝   - a child that reports its errors on stderr, so the result stream
⍝     stays clean and CaptureStderr is the only way to see why a query
⍝     returned nothing,
⍝   - a child that keeps serving after an error rather than dying.
⎕IO←1
⎕FIX'file://D:/devel/mcp-client/src/Shell.dyalog'

Check←{⍺:0 ⋄ ⎕←'FAIL: ',⍵ ⋄ 1}
nFail←0

⍝ One Send, N lines back, ended by a sentinel row we invent ourselves.
∇ rows←h Query sql;line
  h Shell.Send sql
  h Shell.Send'select ''<<end>>'';'
  rows←⍬
  :Repeat
      line←5 Shell.Receive h
      :If line≡'<<end>>' ⋄ :Leave ⋄ :EndIf
      rows,←⊂'|'(≠⊆⊢)line
  :EndRepeat
∇

h←(CaptureStderr:1)Shell.Start'sqlite3' '-batch'

⍝ '.mode list'/'.separator |' are what make each row exactly one line
⍝ with a known delimiter — sqlite3's default output is a box-drawing
⍝ table, which is not a framing anything could parse.
h Shell.Send'.mode list'
h Shell.Send'.separator |'
h Shell.Send'create table lang(name text, year integer);'
h Shell.Send'insert into lang values (''APL'',1966),(''SQL'',1974),(''Python'',1991);'

⍝ --- 1. A multi-row answer, in order -------------------------------
rows←h Query'select name, year from lang order by year;'
⎕←'rows: ',⍕¨rows
nFail+←(3=≢rows)Check'expected exactly three rows back from one Send'
nFail+←((⊃¨rows)≡'APL' 'SQL' 'Python')Check'rows should arrive in the order asked for'
nFail+←((2⊃2⊃rows)≡'1974')Check'the second column of the second row should be SQL''s year'

⍝ Numeric conversion via ⎕VFI, never ⍎ — same house rule as the JSON
⍝ layers use for server-supplied text. Note ⎕VFI returns
⍝ (validMask values): the numbers are the SECOND element, and ⊃ on the
⍝ whole result would silently give you the mask instead.
years←{⊃2⊃⎕VFI 2⊃⍵}¨rows
⎕←'years: ',⍕years
nFail+←(years≡1966 1974 1991)Check'years should convert to real numbers'

⍝ --- 2. A single-row aggregate -------------------------------------
agg←h Query'select count(*), max(year) from lang;'
⎕←'aggregate: ',⍕⊃agg
nFail+←(1=≢agg)Check'an aggregate should be exactly one row'
⍝ Compared as numbers, not as text: a literal ''3'' is a character
⍝ SCALAR in APL, so ('3' '1991')≢(,'3')('1991') and a text comparison
⍝ here fails for a reason that has nothing to do with sqlite3.
nFail+←(({⊃2⊃⎕VFI ⍵}¨⊃agg)≡3 1991)Check'the aggregate values should be right'

⍝ --- 3. An error goes to stderr, not into the result stream --------
bad←h Query'select * from no_such_table;'
⎕DL 1 ⍝ stdout/stderr are separate streams with no ordering guarantee
⎕←'failed query rows=',(⍕≢bad),' stderr=',⍕h.StderrLines
nFail+←(0=≢bad)Check'a failed query should contribute nothing to the result stream'
nFail+←(0≠≢h.StderrLines)Check'CaptureStderr should have caught sqlite3''s error text'
nFail+←(∨/∨/¨'no_such_table'∘⍷¨h.StderrLines)Check'the captured error should name the missing table'

⍝ --- 4. ...and the child keeps serving afterwards ------------------
alive←h Query'select ''yes'';'
⎕←'after the error: ',⍕⊃alive
nFail+←((⊃alive)≡,⊂'yes')Check'the session should survive a SQL error'
nFail+←('Running'≡h.Status)Check'the child should still be running after a SQL error'

⍝ --- 5. Ordinary teardown: EOF is enough, no force-kill needed -----
Shell.Stop h
⎕←'status=',h.Status,' killed=',⍕h.Killed
nFail+←('Exited'≡h.Status)Check'sqlite3 should exit when its stdin closes'
nFail+←(0=h.Killed)Check'sqlite3 exits on EOF, so it must not need force-killing'

⎕←'nFail=',⍕nFail
:If nFail≠0
    ⎕←'FAIL: ',(⍕nFail),' check(s) failed'
:Else
    ⎕←'all checks passed'
:EndIf
⎕OFF
